        },
        {
          "type": "doc_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.423,
            0.32,
            0.629,
            0.345
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "SUMMARY"
            }
          ],
          "level": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.371,
            0.885,
            0.437
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If a number is divisible by another, the second number is called a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "factor",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " of the first. For example, 4 is a factor of 12 because 12 is divisible by 4 (12 ÷ 4 = 3)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.44,
            0.884,
            0.482
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Prime numbers",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " are numbers like 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, … that have only two factors, namely 1 and themselves."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.486,
            0.885,
            0.574
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Composite numbers",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " are numbers like 4, 6, 8, 9, … that have more than 2 factors, i.e., at least one factor other than 1 and themselves. For example, 8 has the factor 4 and 9 has the factor 3, so 8 and 9 are both composite."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.577,
            0.884,
            0.641
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Every number greater than 1 can be written as a product of prime numbers. This is called the number’s "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "prime factorisation",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". For example, 84 = 2 × 2 × 3 × 7."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.645,
            0.884,
            0.687
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "There is only one way to factorise a number into primes, except for the ordering of the factors."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.69,
            0.884,
            0.733
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "■ Two numbers that do not have a common factor other than 1 are said to be "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "co-prime",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.736,
            0.885,
            0.802
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "To check if two numbers are co-prime, we can first find their prime factorisations and check if there is a common prime factor. If there is no common prime factor, they are co-prime, and otherwise they are not."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.804,
            0.886,
            0.869
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A number is a factor of another number if the prime factorisation of the first number is included in the prime factorisation of the second number."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 12,
